From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tim Ansell <me@mith.ro>
Date: Tue, 22 Sep 2026 12:36:58 +0930
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] tcl/board: Kosagi NeTV2 driven from a Raspberry Pi 40-pin
 header

The NeTV2 (Artix-7 XC7A35T or XC7A100T) stacks on a Raspberry Pi and is
programmed over JTAG from the Pi's header: TCK GPIO4, TMS GPIO17,
TDI GPIO27, TDO GPIO22. Which adapter can drive those pins depends on the
Pi generation, so there is one board file per adapter and one that picks:

  netv2-rpi-bcm2835gpio.cfg  Pi 1-4 / Zero, memory-mapped GPIO (the
                             wiring Alphamax's own scripts use); the SoC
                             generation and clock calibration are derived
                             from the device tree and cpufreq with the
                             table of values interface/raspberrypi-native.cfg
                             uses, rather than sourcing that file (which
                             also claims the standard header JTAG/SWD pins)
  netv2-rpi-rp1pio.cfg       Pi 5 through the RP1 PIO (rp1_pio_jtag)
  netv2-rpi-linuxgpiod.cfg   any Pi through the GPIO character device;
                             NETV2_GPIOCHIP selects the RP1's chip on a
                             Pi 5
  netv2-rpi.cfg              /dev/pio0 -> rp1pio, other BCM2712 (Pi 5,
                             CM5) -> linuxgpiod, else bcm2835gpio
  netv2-rpi-spiflash.cfg     the configuration flash through a bscan_spi
                             proxy (jtagspi), netv2_spi_init <proxy.bit>

Nothing drives GPIO24 (the reset line in Alphamax's scripts): the JTAG
chain alone is enough to program and read the device, and reset_config
none keeps every variant identical in that respect.

Every file states both pin-order conventions, since OpenOCD's jtag_nums
(TCK TMS TDI TDO) and openFPGALoader's --pins (TDI:TDO:TCK:TMS) describe
the same wires in different orders.
